1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,990
When an AI application gets busy, a simple question becomes surprisingly

2
00:00:05,070 --> 00:00:09,310
hard to answer. Where did the tokens go? Watchdog gives you

3
00:00:09,390 --> 00:00:13,390
one place to look. It records model requests, tool calls, and

4
00:00:13,470 --> 00:00:18,270
agent activity in a local SQLite database, then puts usage, estimated

5
00:00:18,350 --> 00:00:23,070
cost, latency, and failures into a dashboard. In this walkthrough, we'll

6
00:00:23,150 --> 00:00:27,310
connect the pieces, inspect individual records, and cover the tools for

7
00:00:27,390 --> 00:00:31,150
running Watchdog day to day. Everything shown comes from an isolated

8
00:00:31,190 --> 00:00:35,390
demonstration with synthetic data. No live account data is used.

9
00:00:36,617 --> 00:00:41,017
Start Watchdog with the Kujo runtime, then open its local dashboard.

10
00:00:41,497 --> 00:00:46,377
For an OpenAI-compatible application, set the client's base URL to Watchdog's

11
00:00:46,457 --> 00:00:51,177
proxy endpoint. The client sends its request to Watchdog, Watchdog forwards

12
00:00:51,257 --> 00:00:54,617
it to the upstream provider, and the resulting telemetry is stored

13
00:00:54,697 --> 00:01:00,257
locally. Named upstream profiles let one instance serve several provider accounts.

14
00:01:00,537 --> 00:01:04,937
Applications can also send request and trace telemetry directly, or use

15
00:01:05,017 --> 00:01:10,457
canonical producer batches and supported OTLP traces. These are alternative integration

16
00:01:10,537 --> 00:01:13,497
paths. You do not need to send the same event through

17
00:01:13,577 --> 00:01:14,537
every path.

18
00:01:15,783 --> 00:01:19,863
The overview starts with the questions most operators ask first. How

19
00:01:19,943 --> 00:01:23,703
many requests ran? How many tokens did they use? What was

20
00:01:23,783 --> 00:01:28,503
the estimated direct API value? How long did successful requests take,

21
00:01:28,663 --> 00:01:33,383
and how often did requests fail? The remaining tiles summarize sessions,

22
00:01:33,503 --> 00:01:38,183
tool calls, trace activity, and canonical records. Our sample contains more

23
00:01:38,263 --> 00:01:43,543
than twenty-four billion tokens across thirty days. Watchdog currently displays that

24
00:01:43,623 --> 00:01:47,863
large total in millions. The traffic chart makes busy periods and

25
00:01:47,943 --> 00:01:52,023
quieter days easy to spot, while the other charts separate latency,

26
00:01:52,223 --> 00:01:54,263
request status, and tool usage.

27
00:01:55,483 --> 00:01:58,683
Use the range selector to move from a broad overview to

28
00:01:58,723 --> 00:02:02,603
the period you need to investigate. Choose a recent preset, all

29
00:02:02,683 --> 00:02:06,763
time, or a custom start and end. The dashboard applies that

30
00:02:06,843 --> 00:02:09,723
range to its supported data views, so you can compare the

31
00:02:09,803 --> 00:02:13,323
summary with the records underneath. Start wide when you want to

32
00:02:13,403 --> 00:02:17,163
see a trend, then narrow the window around a spike. A

33
00:02:17,243 --> 00:02:21,323
high token total and a high request count answer different questions:

34
00:02:21,403 --> 00:02:25,163
a smaller number of long-context requests can use more tokens than

35
00:02:25,243 --> 00:02:27,643
a much larger number of short requests.

36
00:02:28,883 --> 00:02:33,363
The Requests tab is the working list. Filter by session, model,

37
00:02:33,443 --> 00:02:36,803
or prompt text when that content was captured, and narrow the

38
00:02:36,883 --> 00:02:41,443
list by tenant, project, status, or provider. Open a row to

39
00:02:41,483 --> 00:02:47,203
see its full record. The detail dialog shows identifiers, model, status,

40
00:02:47,363 --> 00:02:52,563
input and output tokens, latency, and the available cost fields. Related-data

41
00:02:52,643 --> 00:02:56,723
links lead into the session and other associated records. Here the

42
00:02:56,923 --> 00:03:01,363
pricing source and per-million-token rates explain the estimate. These estimates are

43
00:03:01,443 --> 00:03:05,203
useful for comparing usage. They are not a provider invoice, and

44
00:03:05,283 --> 00:03:08,963
they do not automatically reflect a subscription's actual charge.

45
00:03:10,183 --> 00:03:13,863
When something goes wrong, switch to Errors to focus on failed

46
00:03:14,183 --> 00:03:17,543
requests. Look at the error code and message, then use the

47
00:03:17,623 --> 00:03:21,943
session and workflow context to understand what surrounded the failure. A

48
00:03:22,023 --> 00:03:26,023
rate-limit response, an upstream outage, and a timeout call for different

49
00:03:26,383 --> 00:03:31,703
responses. Watchdog records the evidence; your application still owns its recovery

50
00:03:31,783 --> 00:03:35,783
policy. The error rate gives you a useful starting point, but

51
00:03:35,863 --> 00:03:39,423
a single percentage cannot explain the cause. Pair it with the

52
00:03:39,463 --> 00:03:43,943
affected model, the request timing, and the underlying records before deciding

53
00:03:44,023 --> 00:03:44,983
what to change.

54
00:03:46,217 --> 00:03:49,657
Model usage is only part of an agent's work. The Tool

55
00:03:49,737 --> 00:03:55,177
Calls tab shows recorded tools, their status, latency, arguments, and results

56
00:03:55,257 --> 00:03:58,857
where those fields are available under the capture policy. Open a

57
00:03:58,937 --> 00:04:02,057
tool record to inspect its details and follow its request or

58
00:04:02,137 --> 00:04:05,977
session links. This helps separate a slow model response from a

59
00:04:06,057 --> 00:04:11,977
slow database query or document search. In the sample, read-file, search-documents,

60
00:04:12,017 --> 00:04:17,497
and query-database calls appear alongside model activity. These are demonstration records,

61
00:04:17,577 --> 00:04:22,617
not calls to real customer systems. Watchdog observes tool execution; it

62
00:04:22,737 --> 00:04:24,937
does not grant the tool permission to act.

63
00:04:26,183 --> 00:04:29,703
Agent Traces adds a timeline of the work inside a workflow.

64
00:04:30,183 --> 00:04:35,223
A trace can contain model calls, tool operations, shell activity, persistence,

65
00:04:35,343 --> 00:04:39,223
and internal steps. Expand a trace and inspect a span to

66
00:04:39,303 --> 00:04:44,503
see its duration, status, attributes, and recorded events. With suitable producer

67
00:04:44,743 --> 00:04:49,623
instrumentation, events can mark connection setup, first output, a tool request,

68
00:04:49,703 --> 00:04:53,943
and completion. That detail helps answer where time was spent, rather

69
00:04:54,023 --> 00:04:58,263
than treating the entire operation as one slow request. The depth

70
00:04:58,343 --> 00:05:02,503
you see depends on what the producer reports. Missing instrumentation is

71
00:05:02,583 --> 00:05:05,943
missing evidence, not a zero-duration operation.

72
00:05:07,183 --> 00:05:12,863
Sessions brings related activity together. Instead of opening every request separately,

73
00:05:13,063 --> 00:05:17,983
compare a session's request count, errors, tokens, estimated value, and average

74
00:05:18,063 --> 00:05:22,303
latency. Open its detail dialog to follow the underlying requests and

75
00:05:22,383 --> 00:05:26,863
tool calls. In this sample session, eight requests contribute to one

76
00:05:26,943 --> 00:05:31,543
aggregate total, and the related tools remain one click away. Agent

77
00:05:31,663 --> 00:05:35,943
Insights adds summary views drawn from the recorded activity, including patterns

78
00:05:35,983 --> 00:05:39,623
that can help you compare agents and context growth. Use those

79
00:05:39,743 --> 00:05:43,423
summaries to find a question worth investigating, then return to the

80
00:05:43,823 --> 00:05:45,903
actual records to test your explanation.

81
00:05:47,150 --> 00:05:51,310
Canonical Evidence preserves a more structured account of an operation. It

82
00:05:51,390 --> 00:05:56,030
can distinguish an explicit retry from a fallback, show observed first-output

83
00:05:56,270 --> 00:06:01,150
timing, report throughput when the required measurements exist, and describe context

84
00:06:01,230 --> 00:06:05,310
pressure when usage and a known context limit support the calculation.

85
00:06:05,630 --> 00:06:11,710
Cost evidence also keeps provider-reported values and catalog estimates distinct. Watchdog

86
00:06:11,790 --> 00:06:16,510
does not invent retry relationships from timestamps or model changes. And

87
00:06:16,590 --> 00:06:20,590
a successful span alone does not prove the whole operation completed.

88
00:06:21,070 --> 00:06:25,390
Terminal events and explicit references carry that meaning. When evidence is

89
00:06:25,470 --> 00:06:28,750
missing, the interface says so instead of filling the gap with

90
00:06:28,777 --> 00:06:29,550
a guess.

91
00:06:30,783 --> 00:06:36,223
Sources shows where incoming telemetry comes from. It combines accepted observations,

92
00:06:36,463 --> 00:06:41,023
registered producers, and configured proxy profiles. Open a source to see

93
00:06:41,103 --> 00:06:45,503
its identity and the evidence behind its status. Active means matching

94
00:06:45,583 --> 00:06:50,943
telemetry was recently accepted. Stale means it was seen earlier. Pending

95
00:06:51,103 --> 00:06:56,623
means configuration exists without matching accepted telemetry. These labels describe local

96
00:06:56,703 --> 00:07:01,103
evidence, not a remote health check. The Add connection flow provides

97
00:07:01,263 --> 00:07:05,663
setup guidance and registration options. Named proxy profiles can be managed

98
00:07:05,743 --> 00:07:10,063
here without placing raw credentials into the dashboard. Keep credential values

99
00:07:10,103 --> 00:07:12,623
in the appropriate environment or secret store.

100
00:07:13,850 --> 00:07:19,250
Watchdog also exposes JSON APIs and export paths for further analysis.

101
00:07:19,530 --> 00:07:24,090
The dashboard's export action gives you a convenient JSON snapshot. Canonical

102
00:07:24,170 --> 00:07:29,530
JSONL exports preserve structured records for downstream processing and replay workflows.

103
00:07:30,010 --> 00:07:34,250
For an observability backend, configure an OTLP exporter and run the

104
00:07:34,330 --> 00:07:39,770
export worker. Exporters shows profiles and delivery state. Each destination has

105
00:07:39,850 --> 00:07:43,930
bounded queue and retry behavior, and a destination outage does not

106
00:07:44,050 --> 00:07:48,090
turn an otherwise successful proxy request into a failure. Credentials are

107
00:07:48,170 --> 00:07:53,130
resolved from environment-variable references. Supported OTLP data can go through a

108
00:07:53,210 --> 00:07:57,450
collector to compatible tools; Watchdog is not a receiver for every

109
00:07:57,530 --> 00:07:59,290
kind of OpenTelemetry data.

110
00:08:00,517 --> 00:08:04,757
The Backups tab controls the local database backup workflow. Set the

111
00:08:04,837 --> 00:08:09,957
interval, destination folder, and retention count, or create a backup immediately.

112
00:08:10,597 --> 00:08:15,477
Watchdog checks generated backups and records their history. Encryption requires a

113
00:08:15,557 --> 00:08:19,957
separately configured key file. Active backup files and missing or failed

114
00:08:20,037 --> 00:08:24,597
history are separate views. For the telemetry itself, content capture is

115
00:08:24,677 --> 00:08:29,317
off by default, and basic redaction applies before storage and export.

116
00:08:29,557 --> 00:08:34,837
Before exposing Watchdog beyond your machine, configure API and proxy authentication,

117
00:08:34,917 --> 00:08:40,677
TLS, network access, retention, and rate limits. Local-first storage gives you

118
00:08:40,757 --> 00:08:43,957
control, but it still needs an operating policy.

119
00:08:45,183 --> 00:08:49,823
The practical loop is simple. Connect an application. Establish a baseline

120
00:08:49,903 --> 00:08:54,543
for usage, latency, and failures. When a pattern changes, narrow the

121
00:08:54,623 --> 00:08:59,263
range, inspect requests, and follow sessions, tools, and traces until the

122
00:08:59,343 --> 00:09:03,583
evidence explains it. Use canonical records when you need explicit timing

123
00:09:03,623 --> 00:09:08,143
and outcome semantics, exporters when the evidence belongs in another system,

124
00:09:08,223 --> 00:09:12,543
and backups to preserve the local history. Watchdog makes AI activity

125
00:09:12,823 --> 00:09:17,983
inspectable without taking over execution or recovery. Start with one application

126
00:09:18,063 --> 00:09:21,023
and one question you want to answer. The setup guide and

127
00:09:21,103 --> 00:09:26,463
source are available at kujolang dot ai, slash ecosystem, slash watchdog.
